**Rule (Stable dependency admission)** · `rule:decomposition:stable-dependencies` A direct dependency is admitted and kept on its latest stable line. A stable release is a published release that is not a pre-release, including releases below one; a line is the range admitted by a caret requirement, the major from 1.0 and the minor below it. A requirement on an older breaking line, an exact pin that upstream has passed, a revision pin, a line held by another dependency's requirement and a vendored copy, modified or unmodified, each carry in their dependency review the substantive reason for the hold and the condition that ends it; a hold missing either is a defect. The owner of each dependency's admission owes that review, repeated at least at every admission touching the dependency, with the date examined and latest stable release found. (`cor:lint:tradeoffs`) explains why a requirement below one names a minor line; this rule separately requires the reason why that line is not the latest. Existing code and an existing review are not reasons to retain an older line: either merely transfers the unexamined migration to a later consumer. A pre-release carries no compatibility promise, so this rule does not demand it. The owning admission's review, not a release's age, decides admission; no general release-age waiting period is declared. The deliberately dated compiler and forward-tracking server retain their separate policies under (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`) and (`amend:storage:next-major-prerelease`). Moves repeat (`rule:storage:dependency-pins`) where applicable; package-scoped feature, source, licence and compiler reviews, including (`amend:decomposition:protected-package-resolution`), remain prerequisites. Recency alone supplies none of them.

The dependency review dated 2026-11-15 read the public registry releases and manifests. The workspace's exact pins equal the latest stable releases found: rustix 1.1.6, zeroize 1.9.1 and getrandom 0.4.3 retain the mapping, erasure and entropy admissions of (`amend:crypto:secret-home`); sha2 0.11.1 retains the reviewed erasure features of (`amend:storage:scram-residue`); hyper 1.12.0, tower 0.5.3, hyper-util 0.1.21, http-body-util 0.1.5 and http 1.5.0 retain the bounded interface admission of (`amend:decomposition:http-stack`). Exact requirements while current keep those reviewed source and feature boundaries fixed. An exact pin that upstream has passed is an owed move under its owning admission and controls, not a hold, unless a substantive reason and its ending are recorded. None is behind the latest stable release at this review. Criterion 0.8.2, TOML 1.2.1+spec-1.1.0 and Syn 3.0.10 are the stable releases found for the benchmark harness, input parser and source tooling. Their direct requirements select those stable lines; no retained hold is inferred from the interfaces of their predecessors.

The three nrz packages remain at revision 2628abd36f8b700c60a35e5a9134b7f7c8ed2367 under (`amend:decomposition:numeric-backend`). Upstream lists no published release at the review date. The candidate source fails on the pinned compiler, so the separately gated compiler move of (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`) comes first; graph and epoch witnesses also pin exact Work values and certificate lengths at this revision under (`amend:decomposition:numeric-revision-review`). These are substantive constraints, not an exemption because a review already exists. The hold ends with that admission's candidate compiler, closure and evidence review; an unreviewed main revision is not a replacement pin.

The vendored regexml 0.2.2 is the latest published release found; (`rule:vendor:regexml`) states its anchored, budgeted, count-preserving matcher reason and its ending when an upstream release supplies those capabilities. The six wallet trees retain the ownership patches and complete archive accounting of (`amend:crypto:vendored-secret-ownership`). Bitcoin 0.32.102, elements 0.27.0, secp256k1-zkp 0.11.2 and secp256k1-zkp-sys 0.10.1 equal the latest stable releases found; secp256k1 0.29.1 and secp256k1-sys 0.10.1 are behind 0.33.1 and 0.14.1 found on 2026-11-15. The latest bitcoin and zkp dependents found on 2026-11-15 require those older binding and sys lines. That is the line hold's reason; a dependents' release using the newer binding is its ending, with renewed admission owned by (`open:crypto:dependency-admission`). The separate missing fork retirement conditions remain a defect of (`amend:crypto:vendored-secret-ownership`), including the unmodified sys copies retained for archive accounting. No vendor replacement follows from this audit.

The compiler review dated 2026-11-15 found stable Rust 1.100.0 and nightly-2026-11-15, while the workspace retains nightly-2026-10-16. Reproducible compiler/component identity is the reason for the pin, and the separately gated candidate qualification under (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`) is its ending. The server review found PostgreSQL 18.7 as latest stable and 19rc2 as newest published candidate, while the test target remains 19rc1. (`amend:storage:next-major-prerelease`) supersedes the earlier beta-only wording and makes the newest published pre-release the target; dependency review owns advancing it. The rc2 target is owed under that amendment, in its own gated dependency move under (`rule:storage:dependency-pins`), not retained as an admitted older-target hold. Neither compiler nor server moves with the three registry requirements.

**Decision (Numeric model)** · `dec:decomposition:numbers` Counts, identities, multiplicities and order positions remain exact discrete data. Declared finite ledger inputs and rational constants retain exact values; graph transcendental kernels and epoch solvers return outward enclosures. Every binding comparison is decided by an enclosure, never by an approximate iterate. The certificate encloses the evaluated width fence and its residual bound, rather than replacing that fence with a tighter shape-sensitive object. A threshold-straddling interval requires refinement or rejection under a specified indecision rule. Exact encoding of real-valued debits, equality witnesses and the numeric backend remain held until their contracts are settled; an interval is not silently rounded into a ledger value. The designed replacement (`rule:design:binary64-evidence`) removes exact rational arithmetic as a numerical-evidence requirement while retaining the exact meaning of original inputs and discrete data; implementation, carrier migration and publication are held under (`req:design:binary64-admission`).

**Decision (Constants)** · `dec:decomposition:constants` Generate a pinned default module behind a read-only correspondence gate, with no hidden arithmetic in a build script. Check declaration identity, exact literal identity and certified computed-value evidence as distinct obligations. Every public package carries an effective-constants record identifying its edition, announced permitted overrides, effective boundary and reproduction evidence. Locked declarations do not acquire an override merely because they share a carrier with defaults. Illustrative threshold values cannot replace the epoch's computed schedule.

**Decision (Burn interface)** · `dec:decomposition:burn` The records crate owns public data for the burn-amount map, opaque addresses, bootstrap capacity bound and unit-cost projection. A finite boundary sample covers every address the boundary reads; the host carries its edition and required provenance and settlement disclosures. The record makes no claim to implement a burn realization or prove destruction. Its motivating paper surface is {% if publication.imported %}(`[PAPER-def:comparator:frame-inputs]`){% else %}``def:comparator:frame-inputs``{% endif %}. The lineage concept (`spec:lineage:concept`) decides the designed and unbuilt successor for the token, authority, burn or marker subject stated here; this statement retains its landed or admitted scope until that successor is admitted, and the marker and burn replacement within (`gate:design:bite-2`) remains owed by its records, host and development-profile owners.

**Decision (Package and replay)** · `dec:decomposition:encoding` Records, host package and replay remain three crates. {{ require(amendment.status == 'accepted' and amendment.encoding == 'proto:design:encoding', 'accepted publication amendment selects the design encoding profile') }}The external publication package uses the canonical CBOR profile (`{{ amendment.encoding }}`) under (`rec:interchange:decision`), (`rec:xchg:concept-record`) and (`rec:xchg:design-record`), offered as the profile a deployment declares for Q04. This replaces the study's provisional UTF-8 JSON under (`amend:decomposition:publication`); deterministic ordering, exact values, complete references and explicit interval endpoints carry over. Reject duplicate fields, unknown binding variants and incomplete references. Q04 in (`reg:questions:overview`) remains a deployment choice; namespace allocation, the complete field theory and independent byte vectors remain prerequisites for compatibility claims (`open:design:publication-profile`). Representation of non-rational exact values also remains held. The seven-part package contains accepted acts and boundary facts; final ledger and debit vintages; semantic projection and base allocation; tilt, depth, emission, transport and rung verdicts; hull, certificate, enclosure, standing and admission; title, recognition and policy schedules; and formula/constant editions with reproduction evidence. Replay reconstructs these sections from disclosed inputs instead of relying on mutable lookups or hidden solver state. An externally consumed document of this repository follows the interchange conventions (`rec:interchange:decision`). Release support follows (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

