**Decision (Fixtures)** · `dec:decomposition:fixtures` Small computation fixtures live in their owning crates. The verification crate owns the integrated public-input corpus, independent small-case oracles and package replay checks. Certify the mathematical assertion before checking displayed rounding; stored decimal examples are not exact solver targets. A held or incompatible fixture is recorded as such and never counted as passing. No consensus, guild ranking, reward, bridge, CAN or licence-enforcement engine follows from this crate graph.

**Amendment (Served fixture input package)** · `amend:decomposition:fixture-input-package` The admitted pn-fixtures at packages/fixtures is implemented as an outer workspace library for the disposable fixture instance's code-built input under (`amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface`), without adding a computational domain or enabling a served instance. Its one responsibility is the finite served fixture selection with its candidate disclosure constructors, independently authored assertions, explicit synthetic verifier premises and declared resource policy. The name identifies fixture input rather than a verification service. This differs from (`dec:decomposition:fixtures`): owning crates' small computation fixtures and pn-verification's integrated public-input corpus remain test data, while this package supplies a product instance's input through ordinary public interfaces. Its private controls test that input; they do not turn the verification corpus or test-support code into product dependencies. The preserved nine-row computational register and eleven-bite order are unchanged; the additional outer package is recorded separately below.

The four admitted ordinary first-party edges are pn-host for its publication encoder, generation/assertion carriers and ordinary replay interface; pn-epoch for complete disclosure construction and the public verifier-premise interfaces; pn-records for validated constants, rules, policies, declaration values and numerical context; and pn-interchange for the selected Rule payload values of (`amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface`) and, in the private correspondence controls, its public encoding-resource and canonical/shape facilities. The production fixture constructs the interchange value accepted by the host's public Rule payload constructor; the host publication encoder remains the encoding owner. These ordinary workspace path edges reuse their owners under (`rule:decomposition:stable-dependencies`); they select no new registry line, Git revision, external pin or feature and waive no existing dependency or protected-resolution review. The library also has an ordinary direct edge to the existing workspace tracing facade for its own public operation boundaries under (`rule:observe:spans`); it uses the already locked version and workspace feature selection, selecting no new registry line, revision, pin or feature. The dependency review dated 2026-11-25 found tracing 0.1.44 to be the latest stable non-yanked release in the public crate index; the workspace lock holds that release on the admitted 0.1 line. This new direct consumer edge touches the dependency under (`rule:decomposition:stable-dependencies`), even though its version and features do not move, so reuse of the lock does not waive that dated review. The four owner packages use that same facade directly and export no enclosing observation scope; borrowing one through a new owner export or relying on a future node caller would obscure the library boundary and leave standalone selection unwitnessed. This edge does not activate the node fixture feature or change a protected package selection. Validation, restoration, projection, retention, encoding and strict decoding remain with those owners, so no copied validator or second shape engine is admitted. No direct graph or wallet edge is needed for this responsibility; no edge to pn-verification, pn-testkit or a crate's test-support module is admitted. A necessary public constructor absent from an owner returns to that owner for admission, not to a test-only interface. This keeps the dependency direction acyclic and prevents served data from depending on uninstalled test code.

This admits the optional edge pn to pn-fixtures, activated only by the node's fixture feature under (`amend:api:node-package`); no manifest may carry that edge before the public-interface owner's feature and command admission. No reverse host-to-fixture or other production-consumer edge is admitted here. No package's default features or unrelated feature may activate that consumer edge. The feature-off node's package-scoped dependency resolution and executable contain neither fixture data nor fixture selection, as required by (`amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface`). A workspace build that explicitly selects the fixture library or its private tests is not that feature-off node witness; workspace feature unification cannot prove absence in an installed selection. The node's feature declaration, argument grammar, refusals and executable wiring remain the public-interface owner's and are not enabled by this decomposition admission. The present shell's absence of a host edge remains its current scope, not a permission to wire the full fixture path before those admissions.

The fixture library owns operation spans for select, Fixture::disclosure, Fixture::bytes, Fixture::prepare, Fixture::memory_schedule, Fixture::server_schedule and Fixture::replay_memory under (`rule:observe:spans`), with the operation/type census and completion evidence of (`rec:observe:decision`). Use tracing::instrument with the literal name amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface, literal execution_owner FIXTURES, claim_owner ROOT and claim_label amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface fields, an operation literal identifying the function, its exact census source_item literal, and bare ret. The operation literals in that order are fixture-select, fixture-disclosure, fixture-bytes, fixture-prepare, fixture-memory-schedule, fixture-server-schedule and fixture-replay-memory. These are ordinary full-recording attributes, with no skip, result mapper or content restriction: (`rule:observe:privacy`) records the public requested name, receiver and arguments and the complete result or nested error at full tracing. The fixture's disclosure, encoded byte frame, assertions and synthetic verifier state are public test material, not instance-private material. A frame's full Debug formatting, even when much larger than its encoded size, bears the standing trace-level formatting cost and capture-loss rules; neither a length nor a summary replaces it. The library's receivers and results have full Debug representations, including the implemented host replay representations described in (`amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface`). No observation semantics of another package, instrumentation syntax or secret representation is changed here.

The additional public name-membership operation of (`amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface`) is designed and unbuilt. It owns a span with the same literal name amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface, execution_owner FIXTURES, claim_owner ROOT and claim_label amend:storage:fixture-replay-interface fields, operation fixture-name-membership, its exact census source_item literal and bare ret. Its requested name is recorded in full and its boolean result distinguishes recognized from absent; both are normal success, not refusal events. The common subscriber counts one invoked operation and derives the standing active gauge and elapsed/workload samples; the library adds no numerical charge, resource metric or subscriber. Its private shared name resolver runs inside the membership or selection span under (`rule:observe:spans`), because it has no independent work or resource boundary. This adds a designed mapping without changing the implemented census counts below, the seven landed public operations or their observation semantics.

