**Amendment (Shared act profile)** · `amend:decomposition:act-profile` pn-act-profile sits above pn-records and pn-interchange and below pn-host, pn-replay, pn-verification and the planned pn-client. It owns the admitted local canonical formation recipes and supplied-opening comparisons; successor correspondence is held under (`open:identity:act-realization-admission`) for network-v0.37.0-draft.2, ``def:graph:author-commitment-preparation``, ``def:graph:verified-act`` and ``def:graph:ordering-fraud-proof``. Historical v1/v2 recipes and their supplied-value evidence remain edition-selected and unchanged. The designed successor isolates a courtesy module for requests and offered-pair carriage; closure builds and verifies without importing that module and consumes no service result. Graph formation remains in pn-graph; host blinding-value generation and courtesy custody have distinct owners and results within pn-host. The Actor computes its proposal without depending on the host or graph. pn-records owns retained P, C_Y and C_D as typed recipe identifiers and 32-byte residues, while pn-act-profile computes and interprets them; the new carrier omits holder removal facts and D. This preserves codec independence and the acyclic graph. The pure package has no host, database, network, random-generation or signature dependency. Matching an opening establishes equality with supplied bytes, not authenticity. Namespace allocation, envelopes, production entropy, signature and anchor authentication remain held under (`open:identity:act-realization-admission`). The undertaking of the courtesy handshake at network-v0.37.0-draft.2 is not conformance under (`rule:testing:public-record-conformance`): a holder’s receipt is not a predicate decided from the complete public record. Closure determines act eligibility and replayable properties, oracle evaluation checks pinned second-party rules and data, and courtesy covers the transcript, carriage and service. Closure and oracle evaluation