**Amendment (Protected package resolution)** · `amend:decomposition:protected-package-resolution` The protected closure is the normal/build dependency resolution of each protected package built alone from the committed lock. The selection uses default features and normal and build edges, with exactly pn-secret-home and pn-credential-derivation as the secret-handling roots. Development-only reliance is excluded because tests are not installed; reliance enabled only by non-default features is outside this selection. An installation selecting non-default features requires a reviewed row for that selection or refuses. A new secret-handling root requires an amended selection. Every installed executable whose selected package-scoped resolution contains either root is protected; pn-secret-home and pn-credential-derivation retain their own rows as guards against hidden reliance. The checker derives the executable roster from workspace binary targets and their individual resolutions, including transitive reliance through the host, rather than a list of executable names. Each package's dependency sources, locked registry checksums, versions and feature sets are recorded in ci/protected-packages and compared by ci/protected_packages.py; a difference refuses. The gate builds each protected package alone before compiling and running its tests alone, in addition to workspace tests, so an interface supplied only by workspace feature unification cannot become a hidden dependency. Installation builds a secret-handling executable for its package alone from the lock. These package-scoped sets are the protected facts of their dependency admissions. The node's executor may widen the workspace union without widening an installed protected package; every admission reports such changes and separately checks each protected set. Forbidding any wider union would prohibit interfaces the node needs for a property the installed configuration does not have; separate workspaces and locks would split shared dependency review; patching the executor would add a fork without changing that property. A protected package-scoped version or feature change requires its own admission before use. Every derived protected consumer requires its own record; an empty, duplicate, malformed or incomplete roster refuses, and a same-version source substitution differs even when its feature set is equal. The comparison binds lock identity, not compiled source bytes: path rows bind location and name, including a patched fork under the vendored directory, and the compiler wrapper is outside the closure. The tracked Cargo-configuration census refuses source-replacement and patch tables and paths override lists so tracked configuration cannot redirect that identity. Configuration outside the tracked file, including user configuration, command-line configuration and environment overrides, is an installation premise; source bytes at a path and the compiler wrapper still rest on tree review and the installation build witness. The finite fixture (`claim:discipline:protected-packages-retain-scoped-resolution`) supports (`verif:decomposition:protected-package-resolution`) for comparison and roster refusals. The dependency review dated 2026-11-15 admits the generator closure’s Syn patch move from 3.0.9 to 3.0.10 with the registry checksum changed and every selected feature retained. The home and derivation closures remain identical. Upstream’s patch avoids rescanning operands after a failed scan; it changes no selected feature or first-party production interface. The separate package builds and tests witness the candidate closure, while workspace unification additionally selects visit for the parser-only development census; that union is not attributed to the installed generator. The recorded closure is regenerated from that package-alone selection. The optimized debug profile and feature are implemented; their separately compiled shipped recipe evidence is implemented through registered package-scoped artifacts and their focused controls under (`amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile`); installation remains owed. Its non-default observation feature requires a separately reviewed package-scoped row, including the generator, before installation. The protected-package owner keys rows by package and exact default-feature and selected-feature configuration, derives protected consumers for each admitted selection, and refuses absent, duplicate or foreign selections; the default rows and both secret-handling root guards remain. The reviewed debug generator closure is registered, while installed debug recipe evidence remains owed. Resolving the workspace feature union is not evidence for either installed recipe.

